nce  purposes right because there will  probably unfortunately be new variants  for those who may be nervous the virus  is not gone on vacation or is still  around so i prefer to know people who  come in is  tested negative experts say the risk of  contracting covet in the air is low with  airlines continuing to request masking  for everybody on the flight and with  ensuring that people who are traveling  have vaccine certification to show that  they're fully vaccinated i think the  addition of pre-travel testing is  actually the least effective the  announcement could come as early as  thursday abigail beeman global news  ottawa  ahead the potential long-term risks for  children who catch covet 19.  more than 85 percent of canadians aged  five and older are fully vaccinated  against covert 19 but children under 5  still cannot get their shots that's a  concern for parents sending kids back to  school as indoor mask mandates ease as  jamie maraca reports new research  suggests a quarter of kids who get  infected develop long covet  ten-year-old matthew kirkpatrick spent  the summer gaining independence but a  bout of covid19 at the start of the  school year has stripped it all away the  doctors were saying  nothing alone no showering alone you  know if he was trying to learn to cook  no cooking alone  you know no  no physical activities nancy kirkpatrick  says the life-limiting advice came after  her son collapsed on his way home from  school there's this alleyway near my  house and  i passed out and  then i just woke up he had two more  severe blackouts  one was at school where he just fell out  of his desk matthew's post-coveted  symptoms also included chest pain and  dizziness a cardiologist diagnosed him  with tachycardia a condition where the  heartbeat races more than 100 times per  minute a new large-scale pre-print study  looked at more than 80 thousand children  with covid while cardiorespiratory  post-covet symptoms were more rare it  found one in four kids who had the virus  developed some form of long covid the  most common symptoms that were seen were  mood symptoms sleep disturbances and  fatigue and these can be sort of  really common symptoms after anybody  recovers from a viral illness but  it is concerning that we saw those  symptoms continue for at least a month  after somebody had covered claire  hastie's 13 year old twins got coveted  two years ago they've been dealing with  lingering fatigue gastrointestinal  issues and extreme joint pain ever since  it's heartbreaking all of the relapses  and symptoms have really made it  difficult there are a few answers  treatment can be limited and prevention  vaccination isn't available to everyone  matthew kirkpatrick wasn't eligible for  a vaccine when he caught covet in  september you know i regret to this day  putting him in school until he was  vaccinated  a risk some families are still being  forced to take as they wait for an  approved vaccine for the youngest  canadians jamie morocco global news  toronto  next how canadian doctors are supporting  their ukrainian counterparts  it is difficult to confirm the true  number of people injured in russian  attacks on ukraine but the need for  frontline trauma care is dire and goes  well beyond what many doctors are  accustomed to  mike delay looks at how some canadian  doctors are trying to help save lives in  a war zone from half a world away she  was well inflated these are skills  needed on the front lines in ukraine  being taught in how to videos by doctors  at mcgill university using mannequins  they go through trauma procedures step  by step chest tube insertions  cricothyroidotomies and other urgent  medical acts or complex facial  structures before they're translated  into ukrainian  complicated surgeries for less than  ideal situations  the process of simplifying things can be  is very challenging uh it needs to be  more elegant it needs to be more on  point  ukraine has surgeons but like most  countries they don't have enough  throughout the country and on the front  lines to serve all those in need right  now during a bloody war so the mcgill  doctor has targeted medical  professionals medics and doctors who can  help save lives with a bit of rapid  refresher training it's something very  personal to dr nadia demko who has  family in ukraine i think it's actually  the driving force for me is is knowing  how how much they're being affected by  it dr tarak lubani knows from personal  experience how critical urgent cares and  supplies can be  he was working in the gaza strip as a  medic in 2018 when he was shot in both  legs there weren't enough tourniquets to  go around so he had to treat and sew up  his own wounds  i was very lucky i was  i was shot in areas that don't bleed  very much so i lost blood i lost  probably half a liter now he works with  a group called glia which is raising  money to 3d print as many tourniquets as  they can they can't get them printed in  ukraine they'll assemble and ship them  from elsewhere in fact we want other  people to make it we just want to make  sure that they make it safely and they  make it in a way so that it's saving  lives everywhere that's what they all  swore to dedicating their careers to  saving lives even from half a world away  anything we can do to help navigate this  even the smallest degree of support is  is of such great value people on the  front lines in ukraine can certainly use  the help mike drolite global news  toronto  and that is global national for this  wednesday music has emerged as a  powerful symbol of resistance in ukraine  we want to leave you tonight with a  moment of peace in the darkest of times  [Music]  this pianist sat down to play her grand  piano one last time before fleeing her  bombed out family home as she performs  in etude by chopin the camera pans  around the piles of debris she says it  wasn't a sad moment the music helped her  remember all the good times in the house  thank you so much for watching have a  good night  [Music]  you
 

